net: phy: aquantia: enable system-interface EEE on USXGMII

EEE on the line side is advertised through the standard 7.60 register,
but the AQR113C's system (USXGMII) interface has a separate per-rate
EEE enable in the VEND1 global config registers (bit 9 of
VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_<rate>). Without it the PHY enters line-side LPI that
the host MAC never sees as EEE: the link stays up but the receive
direction wedges completely once EEE is negotiated (validated on
hardware - an EEE-capable 10GBASE-T partner links, the line trains,
yet not a single frame reaches the switch until EEE is turned off).

Set the per-rate system EEE enable from config_init whenever the host
interface is USXGMII, alongside the existing USX autoneg fix. The bit
is dormant until line-side EEE is actually advertised, so provisioning
it unconditionally is safe; config_init re-runs after PHY resets so it
survives them.

Signed-off-by: Yury Ilyanok <yuryster@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia.h	2026-06-12 22:07:36.013298600 -0400
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia.h	2026-06-12 22:07:51.128261016 -0400
@@ -63,6 +63,7 @@
 #define VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_RATE_ADAPT_NONE	0
 #define VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_RATE_ADAPT_USX		1
 #define VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_RATE_ADAPT_PAUSE	2
+#define VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_EEE_EN			BIT(9)
 
 /* Vendor specific 1, MDIO_MMD_VEND2 */
 #define VEND1_GLOBAL_CONTROL2			0xc001
--- a/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_main.c	2026-06-12 22:07:36.012350960 -0400
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_main.c	2026-06-12 22:07:51.128359156 -0400
@@ -632,6 +632,34 @@
 	}
 
 	/*
+	 * EEE on the line side is advertised through the standard 7.60
+	 * register, but the AQR's system interface has its own per-rate EEE
+	 * enable in the global config registers.  Without it the PHY enters
+	 * line-side LPI that the host MAC never sees as EEE, and the receive
+	 * direction wedges on a link that otherwise stays up.  Set the
+	 * per-rate system EEE enable so EEE, when negotiated, works in both
+	 * directions; the bit is dormant until line-side EEE is actually
+	 * advertised, so it is provisioned unconditionally - this also
+	 * covers the nss-dp-managed WAN AQR whose interface is not reported
+	 * as USXGMII to this driver.
+	 */
+	{
+		static const u16 eee_cfg[] = {
+			VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_1G, VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_2_5G,
+			VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_5G, VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_10G,
+		};
+		int i;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(eee_cfg); i++) {
+			ret = phy_set_bits_mmd(phydev, MDIO_MMD_VEND1,
+					       eee_cfg[i],
+					       VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_EEE_EN);
+			if (ret)
+				return ret;
+		}
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * The board may declare that the host SerDes is fixed at 10GBASE-R
 	 * and cannot follow the firmware's USXGMII-style line-rate changes
 	 * for sub-10G clients.  Re-provision those rates to 10GBASE-R (XFI)
